---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CyberhavenAd from "@/components/CyberhavenAd.astro";
import PostTestForm from "@/components/PostTestForm.astro";
export const prerender = true;

const curlExamples = `# Probe the endpoint
curl -sS "http://dlptest.com/api/http-post/"

# Small form-style body
curl -sS -X POST "http://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  -d "message=DLP+POST+test"

# JSON body
curl -sS -X POST "http://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"note":"sample payload"}'

# Raw file (single part; good for scripted throughput checks)
curl -sS -X POST "http://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  --data-binary @./your-local-file.bin`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="HTTP POST Test"
  description="Send text and files over plain, unencrypted HTTP to a page that throws them away. Use it to test network DLP, taps, and passive monitors that can't decrypt TLS."
>
  <h1>HTTP POST Test</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Use the forms below to send a test message or upload a file to dlptest.com with an HTTP
    POST over plain, unencrypted HTTP. Load this page and submit the form over <code>http://</code> and the request stays in cleartext, which is what you want when
    testing a network tap, a passive Network Monitor, or anything else that inspects traffic
    without decrypting it. The server reads the request and throws away the body. Nothing is
    logged, stored, or forwarded.
  </p>
  <p>
    Testing something that decrypts TLS instead, like an SSL-inspecting proxy, an ICAP
    integration, or an SSE/SASE agent? Use the <a href="/https-post/">HTTPS POST test</a>.
  </p>

  <PostTestForm />

  <CyberhavenAd unit="leaderboard" />

  <h2>Test with curl or scripts</h2>
  <p>
    The endpoint is <code>/api/http-post/</code>. You can POST any body (form data, JSON, raw
    bytes, multipart). You get JSON back with an <code>ok</code> field, which is <code>true</code> when the body was accepted. A GET on the same URL returns a short JSON
    hint. This route allows cross-origin requests, so you can call it from automation or
    from a browser on another site. It's the only route that does.
  </p>
  <p>Examples (use your own hostname for staging or production):</p>
  <pre><code>{curlExamples}</code></pre>
  <h2>Keeping your browser on http://</h2>
  <p>
    This site doesn't enforce HSTS, so it never tells a browser to upgrade to HTTPS. Some
    browsers upgrade <code>http://</code> on their own anyway, even when you type it in,
    because the site also answers on HTTPS. Here's what to expect:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Chrome / Edge:</strong> a URL you type with <code>http://</code> loads in
      cleartext by default. The address bar shows "Not Secure", which is your confirmation.
      If you type the domain without <code>http://</code> it may try HTTPS first, so include
      the prefix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Brave:</strong> upgrades <code>http://</code> URLs by default, even when you
      type them, and even in private windows (Shields' "Use secure connections"). To test
      cleartext, click the Brave Shields lion icon on this page and turn off <em>Use secure connections</em> for this site, or change the global default at <code>brave://settings/shields</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Firefox:</strong> private windows use HTTPS-First by default and only fall back
      to HTTP when HTTPS fails, which it won't here. Use a normal window, or add an exception
      if HTTPS-Only Mode is on at <code>about:preferences#privacy</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safari:</strong> upgrades automatically when it knows a site supports HTTPS,
      and there's no per-site override. Use another browser, curl, or a script for cleartext
      testing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted small">
    If your browser cached an HSTS policy for this domain from before mid-2026, when this
    site sent one, one HTTPS visit now clears it (the site sends <code>max-age=0</code>). You
    can also delete it at <code>chrome://net-internals/#hsts</code> under "Delete domain
    security policies". Whichever browser you use, confirm the request was really cleartext
    with a packet capture or your inspection tool's logs. An address bar showing <code>http://</code> and "Not Secure" is a good sign, but the capture is what proves it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is an HTTP POST?</h2>
  <p>
    An HTTP POST is a request method supported by the HTTP protocol. By design, the POST
    request method requests that a web server accept the data enclosed in the body of the
    request message, most likely for storing it. It is often used when uploading a file or
    when submitting a completed web form. More info can be found on good old <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Transfer_Protocol#Request_methods">Wikipedia</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Why test over plain HTTP</h3>
  <p>
    Most traffic is encrypted now, so it's tempting to treat something that decrypts TLS the
    same as something that inspects cleartext. They're different controls and they fail in
    different ways. Some of them, like network taps, passive Network Monitors, and some older
    proxies, were never built to decrypt TLS at all. Auditors and regulators often expect you
    to keep testing both, and this page gives you a safe target for it.
  </p>
  <h3>How dlptest.com uses POST</h3>
  <p>
    Here at dlptest.com we don't store any info from the HTTP POST, but most websites will
    store your data. The Worker reads the request body so the bytes make it across the
    network, where your inspection tools can see them, and then throws them away. That means
    you can test DLP rules, ICAP integrations, and network inspection without sending real
    customer data to a third party that would keep it.
  </p>

  <style>
    .intro {
      max-width: none;
    }
    pre {
      background: var(--bg-elev);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      overflow-x: auto;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      line-height: 1.45;
    }
    pre code {
      font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
